      "updated": 1790182215105,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 37.29732845673817,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "text": "To Summarize :",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "To Summarize :",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790182213453,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "version": 259,
      "versionNonce": 1783866990,
      "index": "c0C1",
      "isDeleted": false,
      "id": "q31JLPGFdhqp8JbepSmdd",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "x": -88.26632176744283,
      "y": 26116.558774464786,
      "strokeColor": "#343a40",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "width": 1313.816650390625,
      "height": 1395,
      "seed": 1059221490,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790182215105,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 1,
      "text": "--> There are many Resources that make up your OpenShift Cluster\n\n--> These resources come from various APIs :\n\n    ° You have a standard set of APIs that are \n      deployed with your OpenShift Cluster\n\n    ° The functionality of your Openshift Cluster can be extended :\n        \n        ==> How you would go about doing that is by deploying Operators\n        \n        ==> Operators would introduce new APIs, \n             which in turn would introduce NEW Resources\n\n--> All of the data for your Openshift Cluster is stored in a database :\n    \n    ° That database is called ETCD\n\n    ° How you can interface with ETCD is using different techniques :\n        \n        * like the web console, \n        \n        * you can interface with ETCD using the Api server \n          using like the curl command, for example\n\n        * like the oc command\n\n    ° You can extract the data using :\n        * Json\n        * Yaml\n        * you can even make use of jsonpath",
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "--> There are many Resources that make up your OpenShift Cluster\n\n--> These resources come from various APIs :\n\n    ° You have a standard set of APIs that are \n      deployed with your OpenShift Cluster\n\n    ° The functionality of your Openshift Cluster can be extended :\n        \n        ==> How you would go about doing that is by deploying Operators\n        \n        ==> Operators would introduce new APIs, \n             which in turn would introduce NEW Resources\n\n--> All of the data for your Openshift Cluster is stored in a database :\n    \n    ° That database is called ETCD\n\n    ° How you can interface with ETCD is using different techniques :\n        \n        * like the web console, \n        \n        * you can interface with ETCD using the Api server \n          using like the curl command, for example\n\n        * like the oc command\n\n    ° You can extract the data using :\n        * Json\n        * Yaml\n        * you can even make use of jsonpath",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "created": 1790182213453,
      "labelPosition": null,
      "baseFontSize": null
    },
